# Guest startup/storage diagnostic proposal

The new disk and UUID are materialized and hash preflight passed; no VM has
been launched. The root coordinator
must review the final artifact hashes and exclusive test slot before execution. This is a separate diagnostic, not host-authority acceptance
or a substitute for full original test gates.

VM3's four cancellations still lack a demonstrated cause. The host differential
in `native-vm-timeout-diagnosis-20260921` observed exact child readiness in
437–1107 ms both with inherited `NODE_TEST_CONTEXT=child-v8` and with it removed.
It does not support stripping the variable as a fix. Guest module startup,
filesystem metadata latency and synchronous compilation remain hypotheses.

The proposed guest keeps the immutable VM3 snapshot, including the reviewed
process projection but excluding subsequent journal-capacity/Buster/authority
work. It runs a bounded diagnostic for the three original long-lived fixture
modes (accepted, sealed, supervisor), first on the readonly 9p export. A private
preload measures bootstrap, resolve/load counts and durations, periodic CPU/RSS,
and first stdout. Each child retains the corresponding original 20- or 15-second
readiness bound; stderr is capped at 128 KiB. The driver then runs both unchanged
original journal and ownership files (15 cases), with their own deadlines and
assertions untouched. A separate 180-second outer process watchdog bounds this
diagnostic batch without extending any case deadline.

It then independently copies the same snapshot to the guest's disposable ext4
disk, checks every entry/type/hash/symlink target against the same manifest, and
makes that guest copy a readonly bind mount. It repeats the same three diagnostic
modes and both original test files against the ext4 layout. Both source trees
are verified again afterward. No source, expectation or fixture is rewritten.
The host export stays readonly; only guest scratch data is copied or mounted.
The 8-GiB disk has room for the approximately 447-MB source copy in addition to
the approximately 500-MB guest tree; this is not an in-memory staging filesystem.

Order and cache warming can affect latency. A difference alone is evidence of a
layout association, not final causal proof; stage timings must identify where
the delay occurs. A later reverse-order check would need a separate reviewed
run if necessary. Diagnostic completion and original-test success are reported
separately. The driver reports diagnostic completion separately and returns nonzero if
any original batch or readiness probe fails. Completion is never acceptance
of failed original cases.

The existing fixed VM envelope is retained: 1 GiB RAM, 2 vCPUs, 64 MiB TCG cache,
2 GiB address space, 120/900-second boot/overall watchdogs, 1200-second CPU limit,
64 MiB console cap, 2.75-GiB shared-memory cutoff and 512-MiB initial
nonreclaimable-memory check. No network, host package installation, host mounts,
host cgroup changes, device passthrough or parallel QEMU process is allowed.
Exhausting a bound ends the diagnostic honestly; no automatic budget increase.

Reviewable scripts are in
`docs/review/evidence/native-vm-startup-storage-proposal-20260921/`:
`guest-diagnostic.mjs`, `child-preload.mjs`, guest init and copied fixed-budget
launcher/image builder. Syntax checking and `--verify-only` passed. Materialization created a
new `node_modules/.cache/native-test-vm-startup-20260921` tree, copied the pristine
guest-root independently, generated a new guest UUID, copied the diagnostic scripts
and VM3 source manifest under guest `/diagnostics`, and bound all resulting
artifacts before `--verify-only`. The frozen VM3 export may be reused readonly;
no previous disk or metadata may be overwritten. Exact launcher flags remain
those of the reviewed VM3 launcher, with only the new fixture paths/name.
